02:54:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your grandma is actually probably 100% right about her own baking! Grandmas usually know best when it comes to the kitchen, and there is a very good chance she was using the real thing. The disconnect here is the difference between **home baking** and the **global processed food industry**. Here is why your grandma's experience is completely true, even if the statistics about the rest of the food industry are also true: **1. Pure Vanilla Extract IS Real (and your Grandma probably used it)** If your grandma bought a little brown glass bottle labeled **"Pure Vanilla Extract,"** she was absolutely using real vanilla beans. Pure vanilla extract is made by taking real, black vanilla pods, chopping them up, and soaking them in alcohol and water for months. The alcohol pulls the flavor and color out of the beans, creating a dark brown liquid. Many families have passed down recipes for generations that specifically call for real, pure vanilla extract. Some families even make their own by putting whole vanilla beans in a bottle of vodka or bourbon! **2. Why Grandma's Baking Still Didn't Turn Black** Even though pure vanilla extract is dark brown, your grandma likely only used a teaspoon or two for an entire batch of cookies or a whole cake. When you mix one teaspoon of dark brown liquid into cups of white flour, white sugar, butter, and eggs, it just dilutes. It’s like putting a single drop of food coloring into a massive bowl of milk—it won't change the color of the batter. **3. Home Kitchens vs. Mega-Factories** When food scientists say that 99% of vanilla flavoring is artificial, they aren't talking about your grandma's pantry. They are talking about the massive global food industry. Think about a giant factory that produces millions of cheap vanilla sandwich cookies, thousands of gallons of generic vanilla syrup for coffee shops, or even vanilla-scented candles and perfumes. Real vanilla extract currently costs around $20 for a tiny 4-ounce bottle because it is so incredibly labor-intensive to grow. Food corporations are not going to spend that kind of money to flavor a $3 box of cookies or a tub of cheap ice cream, so they use the artificial white powder (vanillin) instead. **4. A Tradition of Quality** Actually, imitation vanilla has been sold in grocery stores since the late 1800s. It was invented as a cheap alternative for people who couldn't afford the real thing. But many traditional bakers—like your grandma—refused to use the fake stuff because the real extract has a deeper, richer, and more complex flavor. So, you don't have to buy that *your grandma's* baking was fake—it was almost certainly the real deal! But the factory-made vanilla treats sitting in the snack aisle at the grocery store? That is where the cheap, lab-made stuff takes over.
