o They Do It? (Follow the Money)** It all comes down to math. Real vanilla extract costs hundreds of dollars a gallon. Artificial vanilla flavor (vanillin) costs just a few dollars a gallon. But consumers *want* to see those little black specks because it makes them feel like they are buying a premium product. By buying the flavorless, leftover seeds from the extract factories for pennies, companies can make a cheap ice cream *look* expensive without actually spending the money on real vanilla flavor. So, you don't have to take my word for it! The next time you are at the grocery store, just grab a cheap carton of vanilla bean ice cream and check the ingredients. You’ll see the artificial flavor providing the taste, and the "exhausted beans" providing the look. No conspiracy required—just clever marketing!
